21:58:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You can’t see the flags left on the Moon because they’re far too small for any telescope, even the most powerful ones we have, to resolve at that distance. Here’s why: **1. The flag is incredibly tiny in angular size** The Apollo flags were about 1.5 m wide. The Moon is roughly 384 400 km away. The angular size of the flag works out to approximately: \[ \theta \approx \frac{1.5\ \text{m}}{3.844 \times 10^8\ \text{m}} \approx 3.9 \times 10^{-9}\ \text{radians} \approx 0.0008\ \text{arcseconds} \] That’s less than one thousandth of an arcsecond across. **2. Telescopes are limited by diffraction** Even in perfect conditions, a telescope’s ability to separate fine details is set by its aperture. The famous Dawes’ limit gives the resolution in arcseconds as *R ≈ 116 / D*, where *D* is the mirror’s diameter in millimetres. - The Hubble Space Telescope (2.4 m mirror) can resolve about **0.05 arcseconds** in visible light. - To see the flag (0.0008″), you’d need a telescope with a mirror **several hundred metres wide**—far larger than anything built. Ground-based telescopes, even with adaptive optics correcting for the atmosphere, do not come close to that kind of resolution either. So, no telescope currently in existence—or even planned—can make out a flag on the Moon. The best images we have of the Apollo sites come from lunar orbiters like NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, which can sweep just 50 km above the surface and catch the shadow of the flag, but from Earth it will remain invisible.
