21:58:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a perfectly natural way to think—after all, with a camera zoom lens you can keep magnifying a distant object and eventually see it. But telescopes and zoom lenses don’t work like an infinite magnifying glass. The key is that **magnification alone doesn’t create detail**; you also need *resolution*, which is fundamentally limited by the physics of light. Here’s the crucial distinction: **1. A telescope doesn’t just “zoom in”—it collects light and resolves detail** When you magnify an image, you’re spreading the same patch of sky over more pixels or a larger area on your eye. If the telescope hasn’t actually captured fine detail to begin with, all you get by zooming in is a bigger blur. Think of a photo on your phone: if you keep pinching to zoom, eventually you see individual pixels and no new detail. The same thing happens with telescopes—the finest detail they can capture is set by the diameter of their mirror and the wavelength of light. **2. Light behaves as a wave, and that imposes an absolute limit** Because of diffraction, any telescope turns a point of light (like a star) into a tiny fuzzy disk known as the Airy disk. Two objects that are too close together will have their disks overlap and become indistinguishable. The smallest angle a telescope can separate is given by the formula \( \theta \approx 1.22 \frac{\lambda}{D} \), where \( \lambda \) is the wavelength and \( D \) is the mirror’s diameter. No amount of magnification can cleanly separate them. For the Apollo flag (~1.5 m) at the Moon’s distance, that angle is about 0.0008 arcseconds—hundreds of times smaller than what even Hubble can resolve. **3. If you kept adding magnification, you’d just see… blur** You can put a very short-focal-length eyepiece on a telescope to achieve extremely high magnification. But beyond a point (roughly 2× per millimetre of aperture for good seeing), you’re just magnifying the Airy disk and the atmospheric shimmer—a big, mushy blob. You’d never see a sharp flag, no matter how long you stare. **4. Why a camera zoom lens isn’t a counterexample** A zoom lens keeps adding magnification because the objects you’re photographing on Earth are already well within the lens’s resolution limits—they’re large and bright enough. But if you tried to read a newspaper from a mile away with a 2000 mm lens, you’d eventually hit its resolution limit too (set by the lens aperture). The flag on the Moon is like that newspaper, but a quarter of a million miles away. So, to directly see the flag as an object, you’d need a telescope mirror **several hundred metres across**—something far beyond any current engineering. That’s why we can only photograph the Apollo landing sites from lunar orbit (like the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter), where we’re close enough that the same resolution limit allows you to see the shadow of the flag, not the flag itself.
