21:59:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right that the next generation of giant telescopes—like the Extremely Large Telescope (ELT, 39 m) or the Thirty Meter Telescope (TMT, 30 m)—will give us breathtaking detail we can only dream of today. And it’s easy to imagine that with enough mirror size, we’d eventually just “zoom in” far enough to see the flag. But here’s the catch: the leap in resolution needed to see the flag is so vast that even these future behemoths don’t even come close. --- ### 1. Let’s put numbers on what “way more detail” actually means A telescope’s finest detail is set by its diameter. In visible light, the Dawes’ limit gives roughly: \[ \text{resolution (arcseconds)} \approx \frac{11.6}{D\ \text{(in cm)}} \] To just resolve two objects 1.5 m apart on the Moon (i.e., see the flag as more than a dot), the angular separation is about **0.0008 arcseconds**. Rearranging that formula: \[ D \approx \frac{11.6}{0.0008} \approx 14\,500\ \text{cm} = 145\ \text{metres} \] So you’d need a telescope with a mirror roughly **145 metres across**—in space, to avoid atmospheric blurring—just to *barely* separate the flag from the pole. To actually make out the shape and the stripes, you’d want even higher resolution, probably a mirror several hundred metres wide. Now compare that to the largest optical telescope currently under construction: - **ELT**: 39 m main mirror. - **TMT**: 30 m. - The cancelled **Overwhelmingly Large Telescope (OWL)** concept was 100 m—still not enough, and it was deemed far too challenging and expensive even on the ground. You can see the problem: we’re not even halfway there with any funded or seriously planned project. --- ### 2. Why not just keep building bigger? Building a monolithic mirror much larger than about 8 m for space is extremely difficult—the James Webb Space Telescope had to be folded like origami just to fit in a rocket with its 6.5 m mirror. A 145 m space mirror is not just bigger; it would need entirely new launch